package com.ascent.climb.data.i18n

/**
 * English handbook bodies (31 articles / 163 sections).
 * Baseline for every other language: missing sections fall back here before the Chinese original.
 * Keys: hb.<articleId>.s<n>.h / .b
 */
internal val PACK_EN_HANDBOOK: Map<String, String> = mapOf(

    // ---------- h1 ----------
    "hb.h1.s1.h" to "AMS (acute mountain sickness)",
    "hb.h1.s1.b" to "The Lake Louise criteria: headache after a recent gain in altitude, plus at least one of nausea or vomiting, dizziness, fatigue, or sleep disturbance. Symptoms usually appear 6–12 hours after arrival at altitude. Risk rises with height; it is uncommon below 2,500 m, and affects roughly 25–43% of people between 2,500 and 4,300 m.",
    "hb.h1.s2.h" to "HACE (high-altitude cerebral oedema)",
    "hb.h1.s2.b" to "The core signs are ataxia and altered consciousness: inability to walk a straight line heel-to-toe, unsteady gait, drowsiness, abnormal behaviour, hallucinations. This is a medical emergency that can kill within hours. Descend immediately and activate medical support.",
    "hb.h1.s3.h" to "HAPE (high-altitude pulmonary oedema)",
    "hb.h1.s3.b" to "Typical features: breathlessness at rest, a sharp drop in exercise capacity, a dry cough turning productive with frothy sputum, and blue lips. HAPE is the leading cause of death from altitude illness and can progress very fast.",
    "hb.h1.s4.h" to "Management and the golden rules",
    "hb.h1.s4.b" to "First, at altitude, treat any feeling of illness as altitude illness until proven otherwise. Second, never ascend with symptoms. Third, descend immediately if symptoms worsen. Fourth, in severe cases descend and seek medical help at once. Descent is the only intervention that reliably reverses the illness; oxygen, a hyperbaric bag and drugs only buy time. Drugs (for example dexamethasone 4 mg every 6 hours in adults, or nifedipine or a PDE inhibitor for HAPE) must be used under medical direction.",

    // ---------- h2 ----------
    "hb.h2.s1.h" to "Two hard rules on ascent rate",
    "hb.h2.s1.b" to "First, when starting from low altitude, do not sleep above 2,750 m within a single day. Second, above 3,000 m keep the daily increase in sleeping altitude to 500 m or less, and take a rest day roughly every 1,000 m.",
    "hb.h2.s2.h" to "Climb high, sleep low",
    "hb.h2.s2.b" to "Spend the day climbing and training higher, then return to a lower altitude to sleep. This combination keeps providing a hypoxic stimulus while protecting sleep quality — and sleep itself is a key variable in how efficiently you acclimatise.",
    "hb.h2.s3.h" to "Preventive medication",
    "hb.h2.s3.b" to "Acetazolamide is the usual preventive drug, typically started the day before ascent, 125 mg twice daily in adults; starting on the day of ascent still gives some benefit. It is prescription-only; contraindications and side effects must be assessed by a doctor.",
    "hb.h2.s4.h" to "Non-drug measures",
    "hb.h2.s4.b" to "Drink enough, eat mainly carbohydrates, avoid alcohol and sedatives, and keep a regular routine. Alcohol and sedatives suppress respiratory drive and markedly worsen nocturnal hypoxia.",

    // ---------- h3 ----------
    "hb.h3.s1.h" to "Three grades",
    "hb.h3.s1.b" to "Mild: continuous shivering, conscious, able to act independently. Moderate: shivering stops, confusion or slurred speech, clumsy movement. Severe: unconscious, weak or undetectable pulse and breathing.",
    "hb.h3.s2.h" to "The critical misread",
    "hb.h3.s2.b" to "Stopping shivering is often taken as improvement; in fact it signals that heat production is failing, and that the patient is moving from moderate to severe. Most field misjudgements happen here.",
    "hb.h3.s3.h" to "Management principles",
    "hb.h3.s3.b" to "First, get out of wind and cold and insulate from the ground. Second, replace wet clothing and wrap the patient. Third, in moderate or worse cases avoid rough handling and vigorous limb movement — the cold heart is easily thrown into arrhythmia. Fourth, rewarm the trunk core first, not the limbs, to avoid cold blood returning to the core.",
    "hb.h3.s4.h" to "What not to do",
    "hb.h3.s4.b" to "Do not give alcohol or caffeine to anyone with moderate or worse hypothermia, do not let a severely hypothermic patient walk, and do not rub the limbs vigorously. Severe hypothermia is a medical emergency and needs urgent transport.",

    // ---------- h4 ----------
    "hb.h4.s1.h" to "Recognition",
    "hb.h4.s1.b" to "Superficial frostbite: skin pale and waxy, still elastic to pressure, with stinging or burning pain; usable after rewarming. Deep frostbite: skin hard as wood, numb, possibly blood blisters; swelling and blackening after rewarming — a severe injury.",
    "hb.h4.s2.h" to "Standard rewarming",
    "hb.h4.s2.b" to "Immerse in water at 37–40 °C for about 30 minutes, until the skin reddens and softens. Never rub with snow, hold to a fire, use a hot-water bottle, or apply any direct heat source — frostbitten tissue has no sensation and burns easily. Rewarming is very painful and needs analgesia.",
    "hb.h4.s3.h" to "The rewarming decision trap",
    "hb.h4.s3.b" to "If the part could refreeze after rewarming, do not rewarm in the field. A freeze–thaw–refreeze cycle destroys far more tissue than staying frozen. The right move is to protect the part, avoid friction, and descend as fast as possible to somewhere it can stay warm.",
    "hb.h4.s4.h" to "Aftercare",
    "hb.h4.s4.b" to "Do not drain blisters. Dress loosely with sterile gauze and keep the area warm and dry. Avoid weight-bearing on the affected part. Long-term sequelae include cold sensitivity, paraesthesia and abnormal sweating, and may take months to settle.",

    // ---------- h5 ----------
    "hb.h5.s1.h" to "Carrying the axe and moving",
    "hb.h5.s1.b" to "On the move, hold the axe in the uphill hand, pick pointing back and head forward, in the piolet canne position. Its value is that you can go straight into the arrest position without changing hands.",
    "hb.h5.s2.h" to "Four points of the arrest position",
    "hb.h5.s2.b" to "First, get onto your front facing the slope. Second, hold the axe diagonally across your chest. Third, one hand over the head near the pick, the other on the shaft. Fourth, drive the pick into the snow and press down with your body weight, while lifting both feet off the snow with toes up.",
    "hb.h5.s3.h" to "Why the feet must come up",
    "hb.h5.s3.b" to "This is the most common beginner error. Once a foot touches the snow it takes weight off the axe, arrest efficiency collapses, and ankles and knees can be twisted. The brake works by body weight transmitted through the pick into the snow.",
    "hb.h5.s4.h" to "Sliding head-first",
    "hb.h5.s4.b" to "Here the pick cannot enter the snow effectively, and head-first impact is very dangerous. The first action is to roll using your trunk and core into a prone position with feet downhill, then establish the arrest.",
    "hb.h5.s5.h" to "How to practise",
    "hb.h5.s5.b" to "Repeat on a gentle slope with a clear run-out, soft snow and an instructor on belay, until it is automatic. Note how snow matters: soft snow arrests quickly, while hard snow and ice give a much longer sliding distance.",

    // ---------- h6 ----------
    "hb.h6.s1.h" to "French technique (flat-footing)",
    "hb.h6.s1.b" to "All crampon points that are perpendicular to the ground contact the slope at once, with the foot flat and the front points not kicking in. Load is spread through the leg and the whole body, so it is the most economical; it suits low to moderate angles and hard snow, and is the workhorse for long snow slopes.",
    "hb.h6.s2.h" to "German technique (front-pointing)",
    "hb.h6.s2.b" to "The front points are kicked vertically into the ice and the calf works continuously. It suits slopes over 45° and ice walls, but is very demanding and cannot be sustained for long. It concentrates body weight on two or three front points.",
    "hb.h6.s3.h" to "Mixed technique (American)",
    "hb.h6.s3.b" to "One foot flat on the slope for support, the other driving on the front points, alternating. Used in transition terrain between moderate and steep, it bridges French and German technique.",
    "hb.h6.s4.h" to "Traversing and the duck step",
    "hb.h6.s4.b" to "On a snow traverse use the duck step: heels together, toes turned out, so the whole crampon lies on the slope and weight does not sit only on the inner points. Lean slightly into the slope.",
    "hb.h6.s5.h" to "Boot–crampon matching (a safety limit)",
    "hb.h6.s5.b" to "B1 soft boots with C1 strap crampons; B2 semi-rigid with C2 strap-and-clip; B3 rigid with C3 full-clip. Putting full-clip crampons on a soft boot lets the boot deform, the clips lose their grip, and the crampon can come off on a steep slope.",

    // ---------- h7 ----------
    "hb.h7.s1.h" to "Swinging the axe",
    "hb.h7.s1.b" to "Swing from the wrist, not by hacking with the whole arm. The pick enters vertically; a crisp 'thunk' means a good placement, a dull sound usually means a poor angle or rotten ice. Be sparing with swings — a hacked-up wall sheds into plates that no longer hold.",
    "hb.h7.s2.h" to "Kicking and three-point contact",
    "hb.h7.s2.b" to "Bend the knee, lift the foot, drive the front points in vertically. Stabilise both feet before swinging, and always keep three reliable contacts before moving the fourth. Steady rhythm matters more than the size of the move.",
    "hb.h7.s3.h" to "WI water-ice grades",
    "hb.h7.s3.b" to "WI1: low-angle ice, walkable without tools. WI2: sustained ice around 60° with bulges and good protection. WI3: sustained 60–75°, usually good protection but strenuous. WI4: near vertical with short vertical steps, protection may be poor. WI5: sustained vertical, poor protection and very demanding. WI6: sustained vertical with overhangs and very poor protection.",
    "hb.h7.s4.h" to "Why conditions matter more than the grade",
    "hb.h7.s4.b" to "The same route can differ enormously with temperature, sun and season: morning ice is hard and demands precise placements, while midday slush turns to rotten ice that will not hold. The grade is only a reference; judge the ice on the day.",
    "hb.h7.s5.h" to "Safety limits",
    "hb.h7.s5.b" to "Ice climbing must be done under an experienced belayer, with a reliable anchor and a retreat plan. Falls are far more consequential than in sport climbing, and ice screws can rip out under load — that is the essential difference from rock.",

    // ---------- h8 ----------
    "hb.h8.s1.h" to "Figure-8 follow-through",
    "hb.h8.s1.b" to "The standard knot for tying into a harness. It is easy to see and check, and still relatively easy to untie after loading. It is the default connection on glaciers and mixed routes.",
    "hb.h8.s2.h" to "Clove hitch",
    "hb.h8.s2.b" to "Connects the rope to a carabiner and adjusts length quickly with one hand. It is used for the self-anchor at a belay station, letting the climber move freely around the stance.",
    "hb.h8.s3.h" to "Double fisherman's",
    "hb.h8.s3.b" to "Joins two ropes or turns cord into a closed prusik loop. It is extremely stable and unlikely to work loose, but harder to untie after loading.",
    "hb.h8.s4.h" to "Prusik",
    "hb.h8.s4.b" to "A friction hitch: it grips the rope under load and slides when unloaded. Used for ascent, backup and mechanical-advantage systems. Reliability depends on cord diameter and number of wraps — test it before you rely on it.",
    "hb.h8.s5.h" to "Checking habits",
    "hb.h8.s5.b" to "Leave enough tail after every knot (usually at least ten rope diameters) and check each other's work. Knots are the only part of the protection chain made by hand with no factory inspection.",

    // ---------- h9 ----------
    "hb.h9.s1.h" to "The four SRENE principles",
    "hb.h9.s1.b" to "Solid: each point is strong enough for the expected load. Redundant: more than one independent point. Equalized: load is shared sensibly, not dependent on one point. No Extension: if one point fails, the system does not extend with a shock load.",
    "hb.h9.s2.h" to "EARNEST",
    "hb.h9.s2.b" to "Extends SRENE with Efficient (not fiddly to build), Anchored (reliably fixed), Strong, and Timely (built quickly, minimising exposure). It is closer to how a station is judged in real terrain.",
    "hb.h9.s3.h" to "Why No Extension matters",
    "hb.h9.s3.b" to "If there is slack between points, a failure loads the remaining points with a sudden shock — far greater than static load. Equalizing and pre-tensioning removes that shock from the system.",
    "hb.h9.s4.h" to "Different media, different methods",
    "hb.h9.s4.b" to "In snow: snow pickets, deadmen, T-slots — but reliability depends heavily on snow quality and how well they are buried. In ice: screws, checking for honeycombing or hollow air pockets. In rock: nuts and pitons. Never judge one medium with the instincts of another.",

    // ---------- h10 ----------
    "hb.h10.s1.h" to "Step one: arrest",
    "hb.h10.s1.b" to "When someone falls, their full body weight comes onto the rope and onto their partner in an instant. The partner must immediately go into self-arrest and hold. This is the foundation of the whole rescue chain.",
    "hb.h10.s2.h" to "Step two: build an anchor",
    "hb.h10.s2.b" to "While holding the arrest, build an independent anchor with a snow picket, a buried axe or a T-slot, following SRENE. Note: you cannot release the arrest until the anchor is built.",
    "hb.h10.s3.h" to "Step three: transfer the load",
    "hb.h10.s3.b" to "Move the fallen climber's weight from your body onto the anchor. This is usually done by fixing the loaded rope to the anchor with a prusik, or adjusting with a clove hitch. Only after the transfer do you have free hands.",
    "hb.h10.s4.h" to "Step four: haul",
    "hb.h10.s4.b" to "Use a 3:1 Z-system (Z-pulley / Z-drag): a prusik and a pulley on the loaded rope, working against the anchor to give three times the force. It is simple, needs little gear, builds fast, and is the standard for crevasse rescue.",
    "hb.h10.s5.h" to "What the fallen climber does",
    "hb.h10.s5.b" to "Stay calm, keep the rope from sawing further into the lip of the crevasse, try to prusik up, and keep talking to the surface. The team must have rehearsed the whole sequence beforehand — that is the only way it gets executed.",

    // ---------- h11 ----------
    "hb.h11.s1.h" to "Slope angle: the strongest single factor",
    "hb.h11.s1.b" to "About 96% of avalanches occur on slopes of 30–50°, most between 34 and 45°. Below about 30° it is too gentle for slab avalanches; above about 50° snow does not build thick slabs. That means the most dangerous slopes are often the ones that do not look that steep.",
    "hb.h11.s2.h" to "Danger levels 1–5",
    "hb.h11.s2.b" to "Low / moderate / considerable / high / extreme. Statistically accidents cluster at level 3, considerable: conditions look reasonable, the slope looks passable, and many people enter avalanche terrain. At levels 4 and 5 most people stay away, so the share of accidents falls.",
    "hb.h11.s3.h" to "The trio and travel rules",
    "hb.h11.s3.b" to "Transceiver, probe and shovel must be carried and used fluently. In avalanche terrain spread out, cross one at a time, post a watcher in a safe spot, and plan the escape route so the whole group is never buried together.",
    "hb.h11.s4.h" to "Decision habits",
    "hb.h11.s4.b" to "Check the local avalanche forecast and recent weather before setting out. Keep watching for cracks, whumpf sounds and fresh wind slabs. If you have a clear doubt, take the more conservative line. With avalanches, habits save more lives than knowledge.",

    // ---------- h12 ----------
    "hb.h12.s1.h" to "Axes: type B and type T",
    "hb.h12.s1.b" to "Type B (basic) has a straighter shaft, for snow travel and self-arrest; it is strong enough for walking and arrest loads. Type T (technical) has a shaft that can be planted in ice and take a pull, for steep ice. Using a B axe for T work is a clear misuse.",
    "hb.h12.s2.h" to "Crampons: C1 / C2 / C3",
    "hb.h12.s2.b" to "C1 strap-on, for soft boots; C2 strap front and clip rear, for semi-rigid boots; C3 full-clip, for rigid boots. Higher class means more rigidity but also a stiffer boot requirement. A mismatched combination can cause the crampon to come off.",
    "hb.h12.s3.h" to "Boots: single and double",
    "hb.h12.s3.b" to "Single boots are light and agile, suited to moderate altitude and ice climbing. Double boots have a removable liner that can be kept warm at night or dried in a sleeping bag, preventing a frozen liner — a key advantage on multi-day high routes.",
    "hb.h12.s4.h" to "Three easily forgotten items",
    "hb.h12.s4.b" to "Goggles (UV400): snow reflects UV and can cause snow blindness. High-factor sunscreen: UV rises markedly with altitude and sunburn is very common. A spare insulation layer and thick gloves: they must be kept dry, because moisture is the precondition for hypothermia.",
    "hb.h12.s5.h" to "Checking habits",
    "hb.h12.s5.b" to "Before every departure check crampon clips, pick and shaft, harness stitching, rope wear and helmet cracks. Gear tends to fail at the worst possible moment, and the cost of checking is almost zero.",

    // ---------- h13 ----------
    "hb.h13.s1.h" to "Incidence and who is affected",
    "hb.h13.s1.b" to "Altitude illness is not reliably linked to age or fitness. Data show that 50–65% of people above 4,000 m develop acute mountain sickness, and 25–43% between 2,500 and 4,300 m. Studies find older trekkers at moderate altitude (average age 65) affected at rates similar to younger people. Those with underlying lung disease are at slightly higher risk.",
    "hb.h13.s2.h" to "AMS: recognition and management",
    "hb.h13.s2.b" to "The first symptom is usually headache, with at least one of nausea and vomiting, weakness, dizziness, or difficulty sleeping. Management: stop ascending and rest at the current altitude until symptoms improve; over-the-counter analgesia such as ibuprofen relieves headache; if symptoms persist or worsen, descend immediately. A descent of only 500–1,000 m often brings marked improvement.",
    "hb.h13.s3.h" to "HACE: recognition and management",
    "hb.h13.s3.b" to "Usually follows AMS, with altered consciousness (confusion, drowsiness, stupor), ataxia (unsteady gait or difficulty walking), severe headache unresponsive to medication, irritability or hallucinations. Management: descend at least 1,000 m immediately; give oxygen; dexamethasone under medical supervision (8–10 mg initially, then 4 mg every 6 hours); use a portable hyperbaric chamber if descent is delayed; evacuate to medical care as soon as possible.",
    "hb.h13.s4.h" to "HAPE: recognition and management",
    "hb.h13.s4.b" to "Typically occurs above 2,500 m and is caused by excessive hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction. Features: breathlessness at rest or on mild exertion (worse at night), persistent cough with frothy or pink sputum, extreme fatigue, chest tightness, tachycardia, blue lips, confusion. Management: descend at least 1,000 m immediately; oxygen; nifedipine or sildenafil under medical supervision; dexamethasone in severe cases; portable hyperbaric chamber; keep the patient warm and resting to reduce oxygen demand.",
    "hb.h13.s5.h" to "Co-occurrence and prevention",
    "hb.h13.s5.b" to "About 13–20% of HAPE cases also develop HACE, so any sign of pulmonary oedema must prompt a check of consciousness and gait. Prevention: above 3,000 m keep the daily rise in sleeping altitude within 500 m (some sources advise a more conservative 300–500 m), schedule acclimatisation rest days, drink enough but avoid overhydration, avoid alcohol and sedatives, avoid overexertion, use acetazolamide or dexamethasone (to prevent severe illness) or nifedipine or sildenafil (to prevent HAPE) on medical advice, and monitor with a pulse oximeter.",

    // ---------- h14 ----------
    "hb.h14.s1.h" to "Head and face protection",
    "hb.h14.s1.b" to "A sun hat and desert hat cover the head, face and neck. A balaclava exposes only the eyes and mouth and is used at altitude to prevent facial frostbite. A fleece hat should cover the ears and breathe, so the head does not become damp. Choose a neck gaiter with UV protection that covers most exposed skin.",
    "hb.h14.s2.h" to "Upper body: the three-layer system",
    "hb.h14.s2.b" to "Base layer: light, wicking thermal underwear, never cotton. Mid layer: a fleece or synthetic insulated jacket; a hooded version is more versatile. Outer layer: a GORE-TEX shell (waterproof, breathable, windproof), confirmed to work with a helmet; a hardshell for extreme wind and snow. Add a down jacket for severe cold — 800 fill power or more, hooded, non-synthetic fill.",
    "hb.h14.s3.h" to "Lower body and gaiters",
    "hb.h14.s3.b" to "Base: thermal underwear, fleece trousers and GORE-TEX shell trousers. Heavy synthetic insulated trousers for camp rotations; mid-weight hardshell trousers against rain, snow and wind; light quick-dry trekking trousers worn over the insulation. Avoid cotton entirely; choose polyester or merino. For 8,000 m, down trousers should be down-filled with full side zips for toilet access and sized to fit over all layers. Long gaiters keep snow out of boots.",
    "hb.h14.s4.h" to "Glove system",
    "hb.h14.s4.b" to "At least three pairs: thin wool gloves for dexterity, windproof and abrasion-resistant gloves (choose touchscreen-friendly ones for devices), and thick gloves (high-altitude guide gloves, softshell with leather palms). Add SUMMIT summit gloves: waterproof and breathable outer, removable synthetic liner.",
    "hb.h14.s5.h" to "Footwear and socks",
    "hb.h14.s5.b" to "An 8,000 m summit boot needs a removable insulated liner, a durable waterproof three-layer laminated upper, puncture-resistant reinforcement and a waterproof-breathable membrane. A 6,000 m boot should be light, comfortable and grippy; GORE-TEX recommended. Socks should be synthetic (no cotton), knee-length, balancing warmth with circulation on long days; also bring thin trekking socks to reduce blisters. Camp boots and slippers are for camp use — warm, waterproof, non-slip.",

    // ---------- h15 ----------
    "hb.h15.s1.h" to "Ice axe and crampons",
    "hb.h15.s1.b" to "The axe should be a straight-shaft, pointed-head model that works as a walking support and for self-arrest and cutting steps. Length by height: under about 157 cm take 50 cm; 157–170 cm take 55 cm; 173–180 cm take 60 cm; above 183 cm take 65–70 cm. Crampons should be 12-point steel with anti-balling plates, fixed securely with straps or a binding system, and must fit the boot.",
    "hb.h15.s2.h" to "Climbing and rescue hardware",
    "hb.h15.s2.b" to "The harness should be alpine-style (light webbing), sized to fit over the outermost clothing. Belay device: figure-8 or ATC tube. A hand ascender for ascending the rope. Carabiners come in locking (with a gate, safer than twist-lock) and non-locking; carry large ones for harness attachment. Bring a 120 cm sling. Prusik cord is essential — it is the basis of the prusik hitch and crevasse rescue systems.",
    "hb.h15.s3.h" to "Navigation, communication and light",
    "hb.h15.s3.b" to "An altimeter watch usually combines altimeter, barometer and compass, with a storm alarm that sounds when pressure falls rapidly within three hours. A GPS tracker lets others follow your position. A satellite phone makes calls and texts over satellite and needs a prepaid SIM. The helmet must be light, ventilated, easy to adjust, with reinforced foam, and able to take a headlamp; a spare lamp of 400–500 lumens or more (brighter models clearly win in bad weather), weatherproof preferred.",
    "hb.h15.s4.h" to "Sleep and carrying systems",
    "hb.h15.s4.b" to "For 6,000 m choose a down sleeping bag (synthetic is heavier); for 8,000 m a down bag rated for extreme cold. A closed-cell foam pad adds insulation on snow, glacier or frozen ground; an inflatable down mat adds comfort; a liner adds warmth and keeps the bag clean. Summit daypack: 35–50 litres net, ideally with pockets for crampons and oxygen bottles. Duffel: 90–120 litres net, waterproof and tough enough for transport. Dry bags protect passport and documents; medium and large ones are useful for separating dirty clothes.",
    "hb.h15.s5.h" to "Sun protection and essentials",
    "hb.h15.s5.b" to "Sunscreen with high zinc oxide content, SPF 50, protects against sun and snow burn; lip balm should be zinc-rich and somewhat sweat-resistant (SPF 30–50). Hand and foot warmers should only be used in enclosed spaces (gloves, boots): exposed to air they can reach about 74 °C and cause burns or fire risk. Also bring a 1 litre bottle, a 1–1.5 litre thermos, cutlery, nail clippers, repair tape and a power bank.",

    // ---------- h16 ----------
    "hb.h16.s1.h" to "Insurance",
    "hb.h16.s1.b" to "On arrival in Kathmandu you must give the organisers a copy of your insurance policy. It must cover the medical costs, emergency evacuation and other travel protection required for this trip. Confirm the scope and conditions before departure.",
    "hb.h16.s2.h" to "Flight cancellation and extra nights",
    "hb.h16.s2.b" to "Except where caused by the organiser, flight cancellation carries no compensation. If cancellation (domestic or international) is due to personal reasons or bad weather and creates extra nights, accommodation and meals are at your own cost — for example, if the Kathmandu–Lukla flights are cancelled, members pay for the extra stay themselves. In peak season, Lukla flights may operate from Ramechhap (about 140 km southeast); members must follow the adjusted arrangement.",
    "hb.h16.s3.h" to "Luggage and porters",
    "hb.h16.s3.b" to "The inclusions section states the luggage allowance. Weight above the stated limit on domestic flights is charged extra; additional porter capacity is also chargeable. Note that helicopter shuttle weight limits may mean your bag cannot travel to base camp with you, and you may have to wait outside base camp for it, incurring extra nights — a changeable return ticket is advisable.",
    "hb.h16.s4.h" to "Camp discipline and health rules",
    "hb.h16.s4.b" to "During the expedition you must not visit other camps, including those of other companies, and must stay within the designated camp area. The rule has two purposes: preventing disease transmission between camps and reducing safety gaps while keeping the team intact. At base camp, discussing service-related prices is also prohibited.",
    "hb.h16.s5.h" to "Summit certificate and early completion",
    "hb.h16.s5.b" to "After a successful summit you must submit a clear photo or video showing proof of reaching the summit (the summit ridge or a recognisable landmark); otherwise the Nepal Tourism Board or NMA will not issue a certificate. You also submit a self-declaration form and two passport-size photos (white background recommended) for the certificate. If the trip finishes early because of a weather window or team progress, unused days are not refunded.",

    // ---------- h17 ----------
    "hb.h17.s1.h" to "Base camp sleeping bag (6,000 m)",
    "hb.h17.s1.b" to "Designed for around 6,000 m. Choose down rather than synthetic: synthetic is noticeably heavier for the same warmth and becomes a burden in long transports and moves.",
    "hb.h17.s2.h" to "Down bag (8,000 m)",
    "hb.h17.s2.b" to "Designed for 8,000 m, again down-filled. Buy on the comfort rating, not the extreme rating; for 8,000 m the reference is usually a -40 °C comfort rating.",
    "hb.h17.s3.h" to "Mats: closed-cell foam and inflatable down",
    "hb.h17.s3.b" to "A closed-cell foam mat adds insulation when sleeping on snow, glacier or frozen ground; it is simple and cannot puncture. An inflatable down mat is more comfortable but needs puncture protection and a repair kit. Using both is standard at altitude.",
    "hb.h17.s4.h" to "Liner",
    "hb.h17.s4.b" to "A liner goes inside the bag to add warmth and to keep sweat and dirt off the bag. On long expeditions it greatly reduces how often the bag needs washing — cheap and high value.",
    "hb.h17.s5.h" to "Pillow",
    "hb.h17.s5.b" to "The advantage is saving pack space; the drawbacks are heat, movement inside the bag, and poor support when over-inflated. Overall: better than nothing, but not a real pillow — do not expect much.",

    // ---------- h18 ----------
    "hb.h18.s1.h" to "Summit pack (35–50 L)",
    "hb.h18.s1.b" to "For single-day use, prioritising light weight. Prefer a model with dedicated pockets for crampons and oxygen bottles, so sharp items do not rub against other gear. Used on summit day.",
    "hb.h18.s2.h" to "Duffel (90–120 L)",
    "hb.h18.s2.b" to "Single-zip construction to hold all kit for group transport. The material must be waterproof and tough enough to survive long transport, handling and porter carry.",
    "hb.h18.s3.h" to "Dry bags",
    "hb.h18.s3.b" to "Protect contents from snow and water; passport and travel documents must go inside. Medium and large bags are also useful for separating dirty clothes from clean.",
    "hb.h18.s4.h" to "Overweight luggage and extra porters",
    "hb.h18.s4.b" to "The inclusions section states the luggage allowance, which usually covers the trip. Anything beyond is overweight and requires extra porter service, charged separately.",
    "hb.h18.s5.h" to "Baggage may not fly with you",
    "hb.h18.s5.b" to "During the expedition, helicopter shuttle weight limits may mean your bag cannot travel to base camp with you. Climbers may reach Kathmandu before their bags and may face extra accommodation costs.",

    // ---------- h19 ----------
    "hb.h19.s1.h" to "Sunscreen",
    "hb.h19.s1.b" to "Choose a high zinc oxide formula with strong protection against direct sun and snow reflection, for sunburn and snow burn. Reference spec: SPF 50 (for example Banana Boat or Nivea SPF 50).",
    "hb.h19.s2.h" to "Hand and foot warmers",
    "hb.h19.s2.b" to "Toe and foot warmers can reach about 74 °C (165 °F) when exposed to air — enough to cause low-temperature burns. Use only in enclosed spaces: inside gloves, mittens and boots. Never use in ventilated shoes or gloves.",
    "hb.h19.s3.h" to "Moisturiser and lip balm",
    "hb.h19.s3.b" to "A zinc-rich lotion with some sweat resistance helps protect skin from sun and snow; the same applies to lip balm, reference SPF 30–50. Ask for advice before buying.",
    "hb.h19.s4.h" to "Eating kit",
    "hb.h19.s4.b" to "Essentials: water bottle (1 L), thermos (1–1.5 L), spoon and fork, bowl. A thermos markedly improves drinking at high camp and is worth prioritising.",
    "hb.h19.s5.h" to "Toiletries and miscellaneous",
    "hb.h19.s5.b" to "Personal: toilet paper, wet wipes, toothpaste, toothbrush, soap, shampoo, towel, rubbish bags. Miscellaneous: nail clippers, umbrella or raincoat, hot-water bottle, urinal (1 L), duct-tape repair kit, camera, power bank.",

    // ---------- h20 ----------
    "hb.h20.s1.h" to "Important note about this list",
    "hb.h20.s1.b" to "The following medication list was compiled by the doctor travelling with the SEVEN SUMMIT TREKS high-altitude team and reflects real expedition practice. But be clear:\n\n• Items and doses are reference values and do not constitute a prescription.\n• Every member differs in age, weight, medical history, allergies and other medication; a doctor must assess and confirm on the spot.\n• Prescription drugs (acetazolamide, dexamethasone, nifedipine, sildenafil, diazepam, codeine and others) must not be taken without medical direction.\n• Take this list to a doctor before departure, who should adjust it item by item and set your personal doses.\n\nThis text is teaching material, not medical advice.",
    "hb.h20.s2.h" to "Kit contents (team doctor list, reference values)",
    "hb.h20.s2.b" to "Items that may be needed: ibuprofen, antibiotics, acetazolamide, paracetamol, plasters, wound-closure strips, iodine, diazepam, cetirizine, electrolytes, plus prescription drugs for palpitations and anxiety, headache and chest pain, breathlessness and infection (for example propranolol, codeine, sotalol, azithromycin).\n\nAgain: this is a reference range only; actual drugs and doses must be confirmed by a doctor for the individual.",
    "hb.h20.s3.h" to "Blood oxygen monitoring",
    "hb.h20.s3.b" to "Use a pulse oximeter to monitor saturation, especially with a history of altitude illness. Trend matters more than a single reading; record at fixed times (morning and bedtime) and compare with symptoms.",
    "hb.h20.s4.h" to "Base camp medical support",
    "hb.h20.s4.b" to "For altitude illness, SEVEN SUMMIT TREKS provides medical tents at base camps across Nepal's main peaks, staffed by high-altitude doctors, with a systematic process for assessing clients and intervening as needed.",
    "hb.h20.s5.h" to "Insurance and emergency evacuation",
    "hb.h20.s5.b" to "On arrival in Kathmandu, clients must give SEVEN SUMMIT TREKS a copy of their insurance policy. It must cover medical costs, emergency evacuation and other travel protection for the trip.",
    "hb.h20.s6.h" to "Principles of medication",
    "hb.h20.s6.b" to "Preventive drugs (acetazolamide, dexamethasone, nifedipine, sildenafil) all require prior medical advice; doses here are reference values. Do not combine analgesics and sedatives on your own: alcohol and sedatives suppress breathing and worsen hypoxia. If symptoms worsen, descend rather than increase the dose — descent is the only reliably effective treatment and no drug replaces it.",

    // ---------- h21 ----------
    "hb.h21.s1.h" to "Who is more prone to AMS",
    "hb.h21.s1.b" to "Anyone can develop acute mountain sickness; it is not reliably linked to age or fitness. Older trekkers at moderate altitude (average 65) show rates similar to younger people. Underlying lung disease may raise risk slightly. The real variables are ascending too fast, sleeping too high, and individual physiological response to hypoxia.",
    "hb.h21.s2.h" to "HACE risk factors",
    "hb.h21.s2.b" to "Rapid ascent above 2,000 m without acclimatisation; previous AMS or HAPE; existing HAPE (about 13–20% of HAPE cases develop HACE); possibly anatomical factors such as intracranial space, though the mechanism is not fully clear. Unlike HAPE, no specific genetic susceptibility has been confirmed for HACE.",
    "hb.h21.s3.h" to "HAPE risk factors",
    "hb.h21.s3.b" to "Rapid ascent (above 2,500 m without acclimatisation); previous HAPE, which raises recurrence risk; an inherited tendency to excessive pulmonary vasoconstriction; overexertion at altitude; underlying lung or heart disease; cold exposure worsening hypoxia; some studies suggest younger people are more affected, possibly because they work harder.",
    "hb.h21.s4.h" to "A workable prevention rhythm",
    "hb.h21.s4.b" to "Above 3,000 m keep the daily rise in sleeping altitude within 500 m (a more conservative 300–500 m); schedule acclimatisation days; drink enough but not excessively; avoid alcohol and sedatives; avoid overexertion; monitor with a pulse oximeter; use preventive medication on medical advice when indicated.",
    "hb.h21.s5.h" to "Itinerary design is prevention",
    "hb.h21.s5.b" to "Proper itineraries minimise these risks through controlled ascent profiles and gradual climbing. When choosing a trip, make the ascent profile the first criterion, not just days and price.",

    // ---------- h22 ----------
    "hb.h22.s1.h" to "Travel insurance",
    "hb.h22.s1.b" to "On arrival in Kathmandu, clients must give SEVEN SUMMIT TREKS a copy of their policy, covering medical costs, emergency evacuation and other travel protection.",
    "hb.h22.s2.h" to "Flight cancellation and extra nights",
    "hb.h22.s2.b" to "Unless caused by the company itself, cancellation carries no compensation. Extra nights caused by personal reasons or bad weather are at the client's cost — for example Lukla or Kathmandu accommodation and meals if the Kathmandu–Lukla flights are cancelled. If the airline or CAAN adjusts flights, members must follow the new arrangement (peak-season Lukla flights may leave from Ramechhap).",
    "hb.h22.s3.h" to "Camp access and price confidentiality",
    "hb.h22.s3.b" to "No visiting other camps during the expedition; clients must stay in the designated area — this prevents disease spread, protects the health protocol and reduces safety threats. At base camp, visiting other companies' camps and discussing service prices are also prohibited.",
    "hb.h22.s4.h" to "Summit photo or video",
    "hb.h22.s4.b" to "After a successful summit, members must submit a clear photo or video proving arrival at the summit, such as the summit ridge or a recognisable landmark. Only with this can the certificate process begin.",
    "hb.h22.s5.h" to "Declaration form and certificate",
    "hb.h22.s5.b" to "Members provide two passport-size photos (white background recommended) to be attached to the certificate. They also complete a self-declaration form with all information required by the Ministry of Culture, Tourism and Civil Aviation; it can be completed at base camp or after the expedition.",

    // ---------- h23 ----------
    "hb.h23.s1.h" to "A failed avalanche rescue exercise",
    "hb.h23.s1.b" to "Eight students completed a two-day transceiver course on Mount Baker. For the first day and a half they practised locating bags buried in snow, improving quickly until they could find and dig out a target in three minutes or less — better than a St Bernard.\n\nOn the final test the eight were formed into one team, with Tom, a telemark skier, appointed leader. The instructor gave the team rescue instructions, such as assigning one person to watch for new slough. But Tom said nothing about what a rescue leader should do; he simply nodded and walked confidently up the slope.\n\nA skier ran up screaming that a partner was buried under a big avalanche. They dropped packs, listened to beacons and lined up the slope like children hunting Easter eggs. Six people all converged on the loudest signal, so the live target in the small valley on the other side was found too late and died.\n\nThe failure was not technical. All eight were fluent with transceivers; what was missing was leadership: no one took overall control, no one assigned roles, and no one stayed back as a watcher.",
    "hb.h23.s2.h" to "What leadership is",
    "hb.h23.s2.b" to "No two people give the same answer. A workable way in is to start from what people expect of a leader. A survey of hundreds of mountaineers produced this list:\n\n• strong technical skills (wilderness first aid, map and route-finding, weather)\n• care for others\n• good judgement and decision-making\n• being trustworthy\n• good communication\n• inspiring people to do their best\n\nThe definition that follows: leadership is the ability to move people steadily toward shared goals that could not be reached by individual effort alone.\n\nIt is not making rules and following them, but the ability to build relationships; not only technique, but a blend of personality and style; not only body and mind, but spirit and character — a good leader has intuition, compassion, common sense and the courage to take responsibility.",
    "hb.h23.s3.h" to "Hard skills and soft skills",
    "hb.h23.s3.b" to "Planning, technical skill, hard decisions and managing conflict are important hard skills, but not enough.\n\nWhat turns things around is usually soft skill: building trust, speaking plainly, balancing reason and intuition, inspiring the team. Good leaders do not command from rank; they draw on accumulated trust. They do not demand brilliance from people; they keep encouraging.\n\n\"Leadership is never just pointing the way; it is guiding people to do what matters most, in the best way they can.\"",
    "hb.h23.s4.h" to "Leadership can be learned",
    "hb.h23.s4.b" to "Leadership is not a gift or a tool but a compound ability to solve problems, and anyone can learn and apply it.\n\nPlanning and organising are relatively explicit and easy to train; trust and intuition involve spirit and character, are more complex and sensitive, and need repeated practice in real outdoor settings.\n\nThe key point: take people into real environments and let them face real challenges, and only then do you train good decisions. People learn fast when they make a foolish decision, or fail by solving a problem on hope and belief rather than fact; and they succeed when they decide on the facts.",
    "hb.h23.s5.h" to "What an incompetent leader causes",
    "hb.h23.s5.b" to "The consequences of missing leadership outdoors are concrete: a bad plan soaks the whole tent; a hasty decision plus poor communication turns a small problem into a major incident; no one able to handle conflict drives a negotiation to failure.\n\nDo not expect an untrained person to become a competent leader automatically in an emergency.",

    // ---------- h24 ----------
    "hb.h24.s1.h" to "McKinley River: a decision that nearly killed",
    "hb.h24.s1.b" to "In 1963 a Harvard student team climbed down the north face of Mount McKinley. Their heads were full of steak and hot showers — the park hotel manager publicly bet they would not come back alive, and offered to pay for the whole team if they won stupidly.\n\nThe glacial river rises and falls dramatically with sunlight: meltwater swells it in the afternoon, it refreezes after sunset and is lowest at dawn. The basic rule was clear — wait for the water to drop, about 7–8 hours.\n\nThe team had no patience for that. The river was waist-deep, fast, and roaring so loudly they had to shout. Using branches as balance poles, they crossed the first ten braids in groups of three and two. The last pair both went in: one dragged like an anchor by his pack, the other pulled over a gravel bar by the rope toward the main current. Everyone else was too far away to help.\n\nBoth eventually crawled onto a shallow bar. The author's later judgement: this was not just youthful recklessness — with enough decision training to calm the impulse and focus on what should we do, the outcome might have been different.",
    "hb.h24.s2.h" to "First principle: set a turnaround time in advance",
    "hb.h24.s2.b" to "Assess before deciding. Many decisions can be worked out before departure; there is no need to agonise in the middle of trouble.\n\nMethod:\n\n• Fix the latest time the activity must end (back at the car, at the meeting point, before dark).\n• Fix the maximum time the objective needs.\n• The difference is the latest moment at which you must decide.\n\nExample: awake at 06:00 in a complete whiteout. At 07:00 the team has eaten and packed. You must be back at the car by 18:00; retreat from camp in a whiteout takes at least 4 hours; even with clearing weather, summit and return takes more than 9 hours. 18:00 minus 9 hours gives 09:00 as the last moment to decide — before that, you can wait for weather.",
    "hb.h24.s3.h" to "The nine-step decision process",
    "hb.h24.s3.b" to "In the field these nine steps often take seconds or minutes, but the structure is the same:\n\n1. Stop — breathe, count to ten, settle yourself.\n2. Take a first look at the options.\n3. Look for unconventional options — think around the problem.\n4. Get the best information you can on each option.\n5. Use it to state the pros and cons of each.\n6. Weight them so they can be compared.\n7. Choose the option whose pros outweigh its cons.\n8. Execute the decision.\n9. Revise as new information arrives.",
    "hb.h24.s4.h" to "The log bridge: three options",
    "hb.h24.s4.b" to "You are leading a group through old forest. Ahead, a wet log bridge crosses a fast river; there is no obvious alternative.\n\nThe two conventional options are to cross carefully or find another way. Looking for the unconventional gives a third — it is sunny, the river is fast but possibly fordable; about 100 m upstream there seems to be a wider bridge; you have a climbing rope, so a strong member can fix one end to a tree on the far bank and tension it as a handline; everyone takes off boots and rolls up trousers, wades, eats lunch on the far side and dries socks.\n\nRule of thumb: whether you have five seconds or five hours, spend about half the time on conventional options, then deliberately turn to the unconventional. A real example: a raft shipping water with about ten seconds to bail and no container aboard — three people each pulled off one rubber boot and bailed; the boat was soon dry at the cost of two cold feet.",
    "hb.h24.s5.h" to "Weighting: probability x relative importance",
    "hb.h24.s5.b" to "Comparing the risk of bushwhacking with the benefit of the bridge is apples and oranges. The only workable way is to weight each risk and benefit on two axes:\n\n• Probability (P): how likely it is, 1 (low) to 3 (high) is enough.\n• Relative importance (RI): how much it matters to you, also 1 to 3.\n\nWeight = P x RI. A very likely (3) and very important (3) risk scores 9; an unlikely (1) and unimportant (1) risk scores 1.\n\nIn the bridge case, fording won: it had benefits the others lacked (relative safety, a sense of adventure), and although it had more risk items, none were severe.\n\nWatch the subjectivity: RI depends entirely on values, and groups differ. If the team says it will not get wet under any circumstances, the detour is best; if time is tight, the bridge may be optimal.",
    "hb.h24.s6.h" to "Two checks",
    "hb.h24.s6.b" to "Never let any decision system make the final call for you. It integrates and weights, but do not become rigid — if the weights are badly wrong, the conclusion will be badly wrong (garbage in, garbage out).\n\nTwo checks:\n\n• Ask whether what you want to do is within common sense. We can all build internally coherent reasoning on a completely wrong premise.\n• Leave room for irrational doubt — listen to intuition. Much of what we call intuition is long experience operating below consciousness: the sky is still blue but you feel weather coming; you are certain there is a climbable crack above the rock. Crossing a crevassed glacier, almost every step is a choice of left or right, and experienced climbers often use no explicit clue at all.\n\nIntuition cannot be learned from a book but can be trained: keep a record of it, whether or not you follow it — how often it was right, and how often it was not intuition at all but fatigue or fear.",
    "hb.h24.s7.h" to "Execute, then revise",
    "hb.h24.s7.b" to "Execution is the last step, like swinging through the ball in golf. Many correct decisions produce nothing because they were never clearly expressed and acted on.\n\nThen adjust to new information. Conditions change fast: if a channel turns out too deep to ford, that is a good reason to change plan; but if the river is fordable and one person slipped, making everyone hesitate, what is needed is a hand, not retreat.\n\nGood leaders adjust to new information and views, but do not yield to pressure, and do not change judgement to please people or avoid conflict.\n\nFinally: if you did your best, do not regret. There are no perfect decisions. On Aconcagua, with five weather days, one year used three of them trapped in a tent at 5,700 m; at 03:00 the next morning it seemed too bad to try, and at 07:00 the weather was perfect as another team summited. You can only laugh — and use both successes and failures to improve the next decision.",

    // ---------- h25 ----------
    "hb.h25.s1.h" to "Three layers of responsibility: action, thought, feeling",
    "hb.h25.s1.b" to "The traditional definition focuses only on behaviour — this is allowed, that is not. That is not enough.\n\nYou are always affecting your environment: outdoors, at work, in the community, at home. Not only actions but thoughts and feelings have effects. A member in a bad mood, swearing at a bootlace or a lost map, makes the whole team tense; a bus driver with humour changes the mood of the bus.\n\nYour life is like a suit of clothes. Leaders who think they can work flat out all week and then relax completely on holiday are fooling themselves. Think of each setting as a play whose script you help write — if you carry anger from an argument with your boss, your vocabulary tends toward anger and frustration, and the script follows.\n\nAttention to detail is part of responsibility: how you answer the registration call, introduce newcomers, correct mistakes and check gear sets the tone for the whole trip.",
    "hb.h25.s2.h" to "The leader's contract",
    "hb.h25.s2.b" to "Leadership is like a contract — unsigned but real. People expect you to lead, and in return give you the right to do so. Core terms:\n\n• Keep the group safe and coherent — free from injury, accident or going missing, which depends mostly on you.\n• Help the group achieve its goal — a summit, a river crossing, or a happy day in the forest.\n• Help members have a high-quality experience.\n• Lead legally.\n\nIn practice: judge weather, snow and river hazards in advance; manage time to avoid unnecessary night camps; persevere (the duty to prevent the group getting lost is yours); carry enough skill and gear for emergencies; know members' condition — Tim exhausted, Claudia's blisters worse, either can stop the last few kilometres; know where everyone is, with reliable people at both ends of the group.",
    "hb.h25.s3.h" to "Do not abuse the role",
    "hb.h25.s3.b" to "The responsibility can go to your head. Without supervisory authority, never be rude or bullying just because you are the leader — this applies equally to informal leadership, such as travelling with friends who rely on you as a guide.\n\nMore subtly: do not use authority to avoid fair and sensitive handling. If you made a route decision you are unsure about and a member objects, do not hold rank to dodge the issue; take the new information, and if a better suggestion convinces you, be willing to change the line.",
    "hb.h25.s4.h" to "Conflict is an iceberg",
    "hb.h25.s4.b" to "The real cause of conflict is usually not what is visible. Any conflict is an iceberg, mostly below water: a small friction from a week ago, a failure, or something unhappy.\n\nPeople who seem permanently angry, rude, bullying or hostile are often insecure — insecurity that may date from childhood.\n\nNo one asks you to be a therapist on the trail, but ignoring these hidden issues and the emotions around them is naive: they make people defensive, unreasonable and combative. The more sensitive you are to what is hidden, the more effective you are at handling conflict.",
    "hb.h25.s5.h" to "Resolving conflict: build trust",
    "hb.h25.s5.b" to "The key is not to out-think or defeat the other person, but to find and build trust, so both stand in the same place and create a shared, win-win vision.\n\nRafting case: days of rain have raised the river; ahead it splits, and the left channel is unusually fast even without rain. You judge the left too dangerous for a novice group and choose the right. Everyone agrees except Dan and Nora, who are as experienced as you — they think the right is boring and start persuading others to form a second group for the left.\n\nConcrete moves:\n\n• Control your own response first. However rude the behaviour, you control your reaction; lose your temper and you hand them control.\n• Do not label people too early. If you focus on Dan complains, Nora is superior, you create a self-fulfilling expectation that reduces the chance of success.\n• Face the problem — approach the problem person and sit with them at the break instead of avoiding them.\n• Open without threat, starting from shared interests (work, family, sport, past trips).\n• Remember that trust grows from care: listen genuinely, try to see it from their side.\n• Do small incidental favours — Hey Nora, can I help with dinner? A trivial sentence can be the most important of the day.\n\nTrust need not reach godparent to your only child. It can be: We disagree. But I think you are sincere, you want to do the right thing, and I notice you listening — I am willing to respond in kind.",
    "hb.h25.s6.h" to "The follower's duty",
    "hb.h25.s6.b" to "Even trained leaders are sometimes led. Remember the follower's role:\n\n• Give the leader the respect needed to do the job, especially less experienced leaders — nothing discourages a new leader more than a slightly experienced person being cold, critical or superior all day.\n• Comment on a leader's decision only when you are sure it is needed to prevent something worse, and do it privately, not in front of the group.\n• Support positively — lift morale, help members, set an example when your turn comes.\n\nEven if you do not want to lead, do not use that as a reason to avoid learning and occasionally testing leadership. Think of a CPR course: you hope never to use it, but the knowledge has value; and understanding the pressure and responsibility of leading makes you a better follower.",

    // ---------- h26 ----------
    "hb.h26.s1.h" to "Compass and bearing",
    "hb.h26.s1.b" to "Line up the target, the 0° mark and the centre of the needle; once the compass is level and still, the bearing under the N end is the bearing from your position to the target (if N points to 36°, the target is 36° east of north).\n\nFour points:\n\n• keep it as level as possible;\n• keep away from magnetic objects;\n• never mistake the S end for north — that is a 180° error;\n• correct for local magnetic declination.",
    "hb.h26.s2.h" to "Watch method",
    "hb.h26.s2.b" to "Rule: half the hour hand to the sun, and 12 points north. Generally works quickly between 09:00 and 16:00.\n\nMethod: halve the current time, point that hour mark at the sun; 12 then points north. At 14:40, half is 07:20; point 07:20 at the sun, and 12 gives north.\n\nTwo equivalent methods: hold the watch flat with the hour hand at the sun, and the bisector between the hour hand and 12 extended backwards gives north; or stand a small stick upright at the centre of the flat dial, rotate the watch until the stick's shadow coincides with the hour hand, and the reverse bisector between hour hand and 12 is north.",
    "hb.h26.s3.h" to "Shadow stick",
    "hb.h26.s3.b" to "On a sunny day, stand a stick in the ground. Its shadow moves with the sun, shortest at noon; the line joining the shadow tips is straight, and the perpendicular to it is north–south.\n\nMore precise: draw concentric circles 1 cm apart on a 50 x 50 cm sheet, fix it level on a board, and stand a 12–15 cm needle vertically at the centre. As the sun moves, the shadow tip crosses circles; mark the points, join two points on the same circle, and the midpoint points to the centre — this gives the north–south line.",
    "hb.h26.s4.h" to "Moon and stars",
    "hb.h26.s4.b" to "Moon: first quarter, east at 18:00, west at midnight; last quarter, east at midnight, south at 06:00; full moon, east at 18:00, south at midnight, west at 06:00.\n\nPolaris: due north, at an altitude equal to your latitude. Find it from the Big Dipper or Cassiopeia — extend the Dipper's two bowl stars about five times their separation; if the Dipper is hidden, go about twice the width of Cassiopeia's W opening beyond it.\n\nSouthern Cross: visible below 23°30' N on some nights, four bright stars in a cross; extend the long axis from the top star through alpha about 4.5 times its length (no star there) to find south.",
    "hb.h26.s5.h" to "Terrain and plants",
    "hb.h26.s5.b" to "Some features can indicate direction:\n\n• a single plant is usually fuller on the sunny side, with moss perhaps on the shaded north side of the trunk;\n• in northern China, tree rings are often wider on the south side.\n\nThese give only rough direction; cross-check with the methods above and never rely on them alone.",
    "hb.h26.s6.h" to "Map basics: latitude, longitude and UTM",
    "hb.h26.s6.b" to "The earth is divided into 360° both east–west and north–south. East–west longitude runs 180° each way from Greenwich; north–south latitude runs 90° each way from the equator; each degree is 60', each minute 60\".\n\nGrid lines on a topographic map are perpendicular and equal in size, but the earth is an ellipsoid, so latitude/longitude cells should not be equal. The reason is that the grid is not lat/long but UTM (Universal Transverse Mercator). A projection represents the three-dimensional surface on a plane; Mercator proposed his in the 16th century.\n\nBefore reading a map, confirm the projection and coordinate system, or grid readings will not match GPS coordinates.",
    "hb.h26.s7.h" to "When lost",
    "hb.h26.s7.b" to "Getting lost in mountains is very dangerous; build the habit of observing the route. Unless necessary, avoid marking the way (flags, cairns) for environmental reasons.\n\nIf you must mark, three conditions: visible position; clear direction, never ambiguous; unlikely to be damaged soon.\n\nIf lost, do not panic: stay calm, conserve energy, and use what is around you:\n\n• climb for a view, find a landmark ridge or building and fix your position;\n• in forest there are usually paths made by people; following one often gets you out.",

    // ---------- h27 ----------
    "hb.h27.s1.h" to "Six principles for choosing a campsite",
    "hb.h27.s1.b" to "• Near water: camping needs water, so stay close to a stream, tarn or river for access, but at least 60 m from the source. This limits contamination and is safer: rivers below snow peaks usually rise in the afternoon and far more in the rainy season, so do not let water reach the tents.\n\n• Sheltered from wind: especially on ridges, cols and summits (or subsidiary summits). On Everest's north side around 7,500 m a wind funnel can flatten a whole camp and destroy reinforced tents. Also keep tent doors out of the wind.\n\n• Sun or shade: high camps usually favour sun, but in hot weather with people in tents during the day, consider shade — temperatures inside an alpine tent rise sharply in direct sun.\n\n• On snow or ice: fill and compact a level platform. On longer stays the snow under the tent melts or hollows unevenly; lift the tent and fill the hollows with fresh snow.\n\n• On scree: pick the flattest spot to reduce work, remove large stones and fill with small ones; on long stays fill gaps with fine sand.\n\n• Look up and look down: check for dead trees and loose rock above; whether you are on a ridge top or in an open basin (lightning); whether you are on a rainy-season riverbed, a flash-flood gully or low ground; whether you are at a cliff edge; whether the basin could flood; whether you are in large-animal habitat.",
    "hb.h27.s2.h" to "Meili Snow Mountain, 1990: a camp position that caused a disaster",
    "hb.h27.s2.b" to "In December 1990 a Chinese–Japanese team attempted Meili Snow Mountain (6,740 m). The worst mountaineering disaster in the region's history killed 17 people (11 Japanese, 6 Chinese). The main cause was the campsite: the Japanese side insisted on placing Camp 3 below a large snow slope, and a huge avalanche at night buried the entire camp. No one survived.\n\nThis is the lesson to remember above all in high camp selection: comfort and convenience must never override judgement about avalanche paths.",
    "hb.h27.s3.h" to "Zoning and building a base camp",
    "hb.h27.s3.b" to "A large team's base camp is like a town — plan first, then build to the plan. A full camp can be divided into sleeping, equipment, dining, social, meeting, water, power and sanitation zones, adjusted for team size.\n\nLayout points:\n\n• The sleeping area must be very level for comfort; not too close to the kitchen (morning cooking disturbs sleep), nor to the social or meeting areas.\n• Equipment area managed by a nominated person, reasonably close to sleeping.\n• Dining can sit near or merge with social and meeting areas, but should be some distance from sleeping and from other teams.\n• Water: separate upper and lower points — upper for cooking, lower for washing.\n• Power away from sleeping, reasonably near the kitchen.\n• Sanitation downwind and well away from everything else.\n\nOther areas need less levelling; do not destroy vegetation to flatten a social area. Pitch tents in rows where possible, tension guy lines clear of walking routes, and link tents with lines to improve wind resistance. Anchor with movable rocks rather than digging with a shovel or hammering soil, which damages the ground.",
    "hb.h27.s4.h" to "How many climbing camps, and where",
    "hb.h27.s4.b" to "The number of camps rises with route length and falls with team ability. On Everest's north side, a normal team builds five camps above base camp; a Tibetan guide team can do it with three: base camp to 6,500 m, advanced base at 7,790 m, Camp 2 at 8,300 m, Camp 3, summit, and back to base.\n\nChoose climbing camps on two principles: safety and comfort. At high altitude there is no vegetation to damage, so environmental care means protecting rock and managing waste.\n\nSanitation must be in a safe place — do not choose a hazardous spot just for privacy; use snow-block walls or a dug pit. Carrying waste down in a proper bag is best. Always carry a shovel for snow camps, level and compact the platform to avoid collapse.\n\nIf a campsite cannot take tents and sits in a dangerous area, choose a discreet spot where several independent anchors can be placed. In extreme terrain, keep the harness on at all times, including at the toilet and while sleeping.",
    "hb.h27.s5.h" to "The seven Leave No Trace principles",
    "hb.h27.s5.b" to "Alpine ecosystems are fragile and recover slowly. The US low-impact framework — the seven LNT principles — is widely adopted:\n\n1. Plan ahead and prepare: know local rules and special considerations, prepare for extreme weather, hazards and emergencies; time the trip to avoid peak use; travel in small groups, splitting large teams into groups of 4–6.\n\n2. Travel and camp on durable surfaces: established trails, campsites, rock, gravel, dry grass or snow. Use trails where they exist, do not cut corners or create new lines, and never clear a route by cutting trees.\n\n3. Dispose of waste properly: pack out all rubbish, check the camp and activity area before leaving, and pack out hygiene items such as toilet paper. Bury human waste at least 60 m from water; wash at least 60 m away, never at the source, using small amounts of biodegradable soap and scattering the grey water.\n\n4. Leave what you find: cultural and historic sites may be visited but not touched; preserve terrain, plants and natural objects; avoid introducing or transporting non-native species; do not build structures or dig trenches.\n\n5. Minimise campfire impact: use a portable stove, which has less impact than a fire. Where fires are allowed, use existing rings, keep them small, gather dead branches that break by hand, never cut live trees, and put fires fully out and scatter the ash before leaving.\n\n6. Respect wildlife: observe from a distance, never follow or approach; do not feed — feeding harms health, changes behaviour and attracts predators or other hazards. Store food and rubbish securely, and avoid sensitive seasons (mating, rearing) and established watering times and places.\n\n7. Be considerate of others: respect other teams, protect their experience, be courteous and give way on the trail; when meeting a loaded team, move to the downhill side; camp and rest away from trails and others, and keep noise down.\n\nOne more: avoid cairns, flags or paint for route-marking. Use a compass or GPS instead — more professional, and more interesting.",
    "hb.h27.s6.h" to "Campfires and local customs",
    "hb.h27.s6.b" to "In principle cook on a camp stove with gas. If you must have a fire, use a designated fire area, or gravel, sand or rock, with dead branches gathered nearby. In hot, dry conditions be especially careful to avoid wildfire.\n\nNear villages, avoid disturbing residents and keep noise down. In areas of ethnic minorities, pay particular attention to local customs and food habits and get along well — cultural diversity is as compelling as ecological diversity.",

    // ---------- h28 ----------
    "hb.h28.s1.h" to "Getting lost is a leading cause of outdoor incidents",
    "hb.h28.s1.b" to "Getting lost is a major cause of outdoor incidents, yet many outdoor enthusiasts pay it little attention.\n\nIn natural environments, especially unfamiliar ones, the probability is high. Common situations:\n\n• dense scrub or boulder fields — tracks are hard to see and you drift off route without noticing;\n• rain, fog or dusk — poor visibility leads to losing the way;\n• river crossings, the point where open ground enters dense forest, and branching gullies in a valley — terrain transitions are where mistakes cluster.",
    "hb.h28.s2.h" to "How to avoid getting lost",
    "hb.h28.s2.b" to "• Study the area in advance and choose recognisable landmarks (ridges, rivers, side valleys) as references.\n• Follow paths and established routes; avoid cutting your own line or changing the plan on impulse.\n• Be able to describe your position — uphill or downhill, ridge or valley, how many streams crossed, whether terrain and vegetation have changed.\n• Carry a satellite phone on long expeditions.\n• File an emergency contact form, memorise emergency numbers, and carry the essentials.",
    "hb.h28.s3.h" to "The STOP principle",
    "hb.h28.s3.b" to "STOP stands for Stay, Think, Observe, Plan.\n\n• Stay: when something goes wrong outdoors, stay where you are; do not act at random.\n• Think: calm down and assess the situation as objectively and fully as you can.\n• Observe: look at the members, the team and the environment, and look for possible solutions.\n• Plan: combine the factors and plan the next move.\n\nIt applies to getting lost, injury, sudden weather and more, and is the standard first-response sequence.",
    "hb.h28.s4.h" to "Six rules for being lost",
    "hb.h28.s4.b" to "Stay calm and stop first / identify position markers / know how to call for help / wait actively and conserve energy / signal when rescue arrives / handle the situation methodically.\n\nAt night, light a small fire for warmth. Rescue teams rarely arrive immediately, so sleep a little to recover. Build a normal camp or an emergency shelter. Bag food and keep it away from where you stay, so smells do not attract animals.\n\nIf you hear searchers, sweep a torch across the sky repeatedly, or switch on the red rescue signal on your headlamp, to show your position.",
    "hb.h28.s5.h" to "How to call for help",
    "hb.h28.s5.b" to "With mobile signal or a radio, contact the police (110), ambulance (120) or a local rescue team — team contacts are listed on the China Mountaineering Association accident platform (cmasports.cn).\n\nWithout signal or radio, send someone for help, preferably two people together.\n\nGive accurate information: your position, or the terrain you crossed and what you saw — uphill or downhill, ridge or valley, how many streams, whether terrain and vegetation changed.\n\nThe messengers must mark the route carefully so searchers can follow it. If you move to a safer place, make sure rescuers can still find you:\n\n• tie bright cloth or gear to branches, or place it on a ridge;\n• when you hear searchers, blow a whistle, strike stones, bang a pot or shout.",
    "hb.h28.s6.h" to "The ten outdoor essentials",
    "hb.h28.s6.b" to "The carry-always list should include at least:\n\n• extra clothing (windproof shell, jacket or insulation)\n• rain protection (pack cover, raincoat or umbrella)\n• a knife (light and multi-function)\n• matches and a lighter or ferro rod (ideally two different ignition sources)\n• headlamp or torch with spare batteries\n• first-aid kit\n• extra food and water\n• sun protection and insulation\n• map and compass (or GPS)\n• emergency shelter (such as a survival blanket)\n\nIf any of the five — water, shelter, body warmth, will to survive, food — is missing, you or your team are already at risk. Act: drink enough and ration food sensibly (food is the least urgent; with warmth, rest and water people last much longer); conserve energy and avoid pointless exertion; hold on to the will to survive and master fear and panic.",
    "hb.h28.s7.h" to "Stay warm while waiting for rescue",
    "hb.h28.s7.b" to "• Find or build a shelter out of wind and rain; choose ground safe from flood and rockfall.\n• Insulate yourself from cold, wet ground with a mat or vegetation.\n• Cover yourself with dry vegetation (fern fronds, grass) for an extra layer of warmth and insulation.",

    // ---------- h29 ----------
    "hb.h29.s1.h" to "Dehydration: the quiet driver of bad judgement",
    "hb.h29.s1.b" to "Dehydration causes discomfort and reduces judgement and coordination, contributes to altitude illness, hypothermia and heat illness, and in severe cases kills.\n\nWith body temperature normal, a person survives only 3–4 days without water. The body uses about 2.5 L a day, lost through urine (about 1,400 ml), breathing (about 400 ml), sweat (about 600 ml) and faeces (about 100 ml), and it must be replaced by drinking and food.\n\nMain symptoms: thirst, reduced urine, darker urine, wrinkled skin, exhaustion, loss of appetite.\n\nPrevention: match intake to sweating, taking small amounts of electrolyte drink before, during and after activity. The mnemonic: drink in time / add electrolytes / small amounts often / watch for little and dark urine.",
    "hb.h29.s2.h" to "Four grades of overheating",
    "hb.h29.s2.b" to "Overheating progresses: feeling hot → heat exhaustion → heat stroke → hyponatraemia.\n\nCauses: heat, unsuitable clothing, lack of water, over-fatigue, long exposure, poor sleep.\n\n• Heat exhaustion: poor concentration, reduced awareness, slow movement, skin slightly hot, often heavy sweating.\n• Heat stroke: body temperature beyond what organs tolerate, often above 40.5 °C; reduced consciousness, confusion or coma; skin hot, flushed or pale; convulsions in severe cases.\n• Hyponatraemia: excessive fluid intake without excretion lowers electrolyte concentration. Nausea, headache, weakness, reduced awareness but near-normal temperature, convulsions in severe cases.\n\nNote the key difference from heat stroke: in hyponatraemia temperature is near normal. That means more water is not always better — be careful with over-drinking.",
    "hb.h29.s3.h" to "Four rules for handling overheating",
    "hb.h29.s3.b" to "Move out of the heat and loosen clothing / use misted water to cool / spray the trunk / combine with airflow.\n\nIf thirsty, give fluids, but watch for over-drinking. The core approach: move the patient to shade, loosen clothing, and cool the trunk with misted water and airflow.",
    "hb.h29.s4.h" to "Preventing overheating",
    "hb.h29.s4.b" to "• Plan activity sensibly: start early and avoid the midday heat; sleep enough beforehand and do not set out stressed or in a bad mood.\n• Cool the head and shed heat briefly: wear suitable clothing; wet your hat to cool the head on the move; when you reach shade, take the hat off briefly to dump heat.\n• Rest in shade away from direct sun with good airflow; at breaks drop the pack, remove the hat, open cuffs and collar, and roll up trousers to cool quickly.",
    "hb.h29.s5.h" to "Blisters: prevention and treatment",
    "hb.h29.s5.b" to "Cause: pressure and friction between foot and shoe, worsened by damp and relative heat. Friction causes blisters on the sole or outside of the foot; sweat softens the skin and raises friction, making blisters more likely. They are not heat burns, but higher skin temperature speeds their formation.\n\nPrevention: socks reduce blisters; talc, prickly-heat powder, antiperspirant spray or petroleum jelly keep feet dry and cut friction.\n\nTreatment:\n\n• disinfect the needle before piercing (heat it red or wipe with an alcohol pad);\n• drain large or likely-to-burst blisters to release fluid;\n• make a ring of soft cloth around the blister to absorb pressure and friction;\n• a combination antibiotic ointment reduces infection; cover simply with a plaster or gauze, replace gauze when wet, and keep the area ventilated.",
    "hb.h29.s6.h" to "Sunburn: treatment and prevention",
    "hb.h29.s6.b" to "Excess UV dilates capillaries under the skin, bringing more blood to the surface, so the skin reddens and heats.\n\nPrevention: apply sunscreen suited to the weather and altitude (generally SPF above 30); wear UV-protective clothing, a sun hat, sunglasses and a headscarf.\n\nTreatment:\n\n• analgesia (such as aspirin) for the redness and pain of mild to moderate sunburn;\n• cool compresses, or ice wrapped in cloth — never ice directly on skin;\n• wash gently with mild soap and apply a soothing lotion or aloe gel;\n• dust with talc to reduce friction from sleeping bag or clothing at night.\n\nBackground: at 6,500 m relative humidity is about one tenth of sea level, so fluid needs are much higher; at 4,000 m UV intensity is 2–3 times sea level, and snow reflection makes it stronger still.",
    "hb.h29.s7.h" to "Treating burns",
    "hb.h29.s7.b" to "Do not pierce blisters unless necessary. Irrigate the wound with clean water and cover with petroleum-jelly gauze so the injured skin does not adhere.",
    "hb.h29.s8.h" to "A small outdoor emergency kit",
    "hb.h29.s8.b" to "For trips of more than two days, carry a small kit of common emergency medicines:\n\n• Oral: cold remedies, gastrointestinal remedies, analgesics, antihistamines, antibiotics (skin, respiratory, digestive) — ask a doctor for specific advice.\n• Topical: Yunnan Baiyao, plasters, bandages (haemostatic bandage recommended), tape, red mercurochrome, hydrogen peroxide. A small bottle of essential balm and two bottles of eye drops (also useful for irrigating wounds) often prove unexpectedly handy.",

    // ---------- h30 ----------
    "hb.h30.s1.h" to "The 4-3-3 principle: allocating resources",
    "hb.h30.s1.b" to "Common outdoor mistakes: drinking a whole day's water in hot weather; eating tomorrow's food at one trail meal after a long climb; setting out without a fitness base.\n\nThe 4-3-3 principle allocates reserves of fitness, food and water as follows:\n\n• 40% used on the way up\n• 30% used on the way down\n• 30% still in reserve when the activity ends\n\nThe overall reserve usually covers fitness, mental energy, food, water and backup supplies such as batteries.\n\nIts value is that it separates still moving from still having margin — the summit is never the finish; returning safely is.",
    "hb.h30.s2.h" to "The three-new rule",
    "hb.h30.s2.b" to "People seek stronger experiences by choosing unfamiliar places or routes. But as leader or individual, test every trip against the three-new rule before setting out.\n\nThree new things: a new route, new partners, new equipment (or a new environment or season). Together they multiply uncertainty sharply.\n\nThe rule does not forbid going out; it requires that the three do not all appear at once — keep at least one thing familiar.",
    "hb.h30.s3.h" to "The STOP principle",
    "hb.h30.s3.b" to "Widely used at home and abroad, it gives an effective response when something goes wrong:\n\n• S = Stay: stay put; do not act at random.\n• T = Think: calm down and judge accurately.\n• O = Observe: look at the situation and find options.\n• P = Plan: combine the factors and plan.\n\nThe order: stop where you are; think calmly and assess as objectively as possible; observe members, team and environment for possible solutions; then combine the factors and plan the next move.",
    "hb.h30.s4.h" to "Risk management: consequence x likelihood",
    "hb.h30.s4.b" to "Risk is the probability of an incident, expressed through two measures: consequence and likelihood. Assess each separately by risk category, calculate a risk value, and combine them into an overall level.\n\nConsequence scale (1–10,000, using personal injury):\n\n• very low: graze, bruise, bump, no recovery time\n• low: contusion or sprain, about 1 week to recover\n• medium: transfusion or fracture, 1–6 weeks\n• high: hospital treatment, more than 6 weeks\n• very high: permanent disability or death\n\nLikelihood scale (0.1–1.0):\n\n• rare: once in 10 years, or 1 in 10 trips\n• unlikely: once in 5 years, or 1 in 5 trips\n• possible: once in 3 years, or 1 in 3 trips\n• likely: once a year, or 2 in 3 trips\n• almost certain: several times a year, or nearly every trip\n\nCombining the two gives an intuitive risk level. The zone with very high consequence and a likelihood you believe is very low deserves the most caution — the familiar thoughts, it will not happen to us and our luck is not that bad, are exactly the optimism bias to watch for. Give that zone particular attention and mitigation.",
    "hb.h30.s5.h" to "Risk identification: list the hazards one by one",
    "hb.h30.s5.b" to "Risk identification means finding each hazard and clarifying where injury, financial loss, reputational or market harm could arise in a climbing activity. Personal injury is the priority.\n\nCommon categories: slips and falls, falls from height; one's own condition, knowledge and training; acclimatisation problems; rockfall or icefall from other parties; cornice collapse; avalanches; altitude illness; frostbite; hypothermia; sunburn.\n\nIt can also be done segment by segment. On the Yuzhu north-face Glacier 2 route, for example, list risks for each leg (Xidatan to BC, 20–40 minutes by vehicle, 4,160–4,470 m; BC to the boot-change point, 2–3 hours, 4,470–4,670 m; boot change to C1, 2–3 hours, 4,670–5,100 m; C1 to the 5,250 m summit, 40–90 minutes, 5,100–5,250 m).\n\nSegment-by-segment is more effective than a blanket assessment: it turns an abstract there is risk into a checklist you can verify.",
    "hb.h30.s6.h" to "Three rules for a safe camp",
    "hb.h30.s6.b" to "Prepare in advance / choose the site methodically · anticipate hazards / focus protection · stay alert · be ready to leave at any time.\n\nThe last phrase is often forgotten: risk assessment is not a one-off. Weather, snow and team condition change, and whether the camp remains usable must be re-judged continuously.",

    // ---------- h31 ----------
    "hb.h31.s1.h" to "The three energy substrates",
    "hb.h31.s1.b" to "Carbohydrate is a major structural component of cells and the main energy substrate. In moderate aerobic exercise the body runs mainly on carbohydrate; in anaerobic work it runs almost entirely on it. It is stored as blood glucose, muscle glycogen and liver glycogen.\n\nFat: for many athletes an inefficient fuel, but in prolonged low-intensity work — including mountaineering — more energy must come from fat rather than carbohydrate. That does not make carbohydrate stores unimportant: fat breakdown depends on a continuous supply of glycogen and blood glucose. If they approach depletion, fat metabolism slows sharply.\n\nProtein: many climbers overlook it as a fuel. In fact adequate protein underpins strength and recovery. The reference intake is 0.8 g per kg per day, but athletes training or climbing hard may need substantially more.",
    "hb.h31.s2.h" to "Training raises the share supplied by fat",
    "hb.h31.s2.b" to "Aerobic work below about 75% of anaerobic-threshold heart rate improves fat metabolism. Well-trained climbers can take 70–80% of their energy from fat over long climbs.\n\nThat matters on long routes: with the same supplies, you can go further.\n\nBut watch the switch: short explosive efforts (moving fast over a steep step) shift supply back to carbohydrate. Trained people switch quickly. If glycogen is depleted soon after starting, be very careful — muscle glycogen cannot be resynthesised during exercise. Climbing needs glycogen both for explosive effort and to support fat metabolism, so pace sensibly.",
    "hb.h31.s3.h" to "Branched-chain amino acids and ammonia",
    "hb.h31.s3.b" to "Branched-chain amino acids (leucine, isoleucine, valine) make up about 20% of muscle protein and are essential for endurance and power output.\n\nWhen their blood levels fall below a threshold, the body breaks down muscle protein to replace them — it begins to eat its own muscle.\n\nAny shortfall in glucose and fatty acids activates this breakdown; in some situations protein catabolism becomes the main energy source. It destroys muscle protein structure and produces large amounts of ammonia, which is toxic to cells. High ammonia impairs performance and also thinking — especially dangerous at altitude, where decisions must be made continuously.\n\nHave you noticed the ammonia-like smell after a hard day's climbing? That is the by-product of burning muscle protein.",
    "hb.h31.s4.h" to "Fuelling strategy on the climb",
    "hb.h31.s4.b" to "Long alpine climbs need a full nutrition strategy. Two core rules:\n\n1. Keep intensity in the range where fat metabolism works, so glycogen is spared as far as possible.\n2. Pay attention to specific nutrients:\n\n• enough carbohydrate to maintain blood glucose;\n• enough branched-chain amino acids to limit muscle breakdown;\n• nutrients that neutralise ammonia, so protein metabolism does not impair performance;\n• antioxidants (vitamin C, vitamin E) during the climb and at rest.\n\nProtein: animal and plant sources both work, but dairy and eggs are used more efficiently than plant protein, so less is needed. Excess protein does not improve performance — the body knows what it needs and converts or excretes the rest.",
    "hb.h31.s5.h" to "Trail food: bars and gels",
    "hb.h31.s5.b" to "Early climbers used energy bars (Snickers, PowerBar) as trail food. They are energy-dense, but need at least half a litre of water straight afterwards for proper absorption; and digesting a bar increases blood flow to the gut, which can hurt performance.\n\nGels (for example GU, about 25 g carbohydrate per sachet) are easier to use and absorb: a few mouthfuls of water with the gel brings the carbohydrate concentration into the 4–8% range that empties from the stomach fastest.\n\nGels also contain leucine and valine — both branched-chain amino acids needed for exercise and recovery — plus a little caffeine, which supports fat metabolism and alertness.\n\nNote: caffeine constricts blood vessels; use it cautiously and individually.",
    "hb.h31.s6.h" to "Special demands at altitude",
    "hb.h31.s6.b" to "Gaining altitude changes fuelling through two variables:\n\n• Lower humidity: at 6,500 m relative humidity is about one tenth of sea level, so insensible losses from breathing and skin rise sharply and fluid intake must increase.\n• Lower appetite: appetite commonly falls at altitude while energy expenditure rises.\n\nSo the rule at altitude is: small amounts often, mostly liquid or semi-liquid, and do not wait until hungry. Treat eating as a scheduled task, not something you do when the body asks."
)
